# PHASE 8 — COMPARE MODELS AND OPTIMISATIONS

Only after the instrumentation and benchmark harness are reliable, run controlled comparisons.

Compare these configurations independently:

1. Current production pipeline.
2. Current model with reference reuse and reduced redundant context.
3. JSON-first extraction with the current grading model.
4. JSON-first extraction with a lower-cost Gemini grading model.
5. JSON-first extraction with question-group grading and compact prompts.
6. JSON-first extraction with selective escalation.
7. The best combined configuration.

Change one major factor at a time initially so that its effect can be measured.

For each configuration, report actual cost when real API data exists. Otherwise, report only the offline test results and identify real cost as unmeasured.

Do not claim that JSON, batching, caching or a cheaper model necessarily lowers total cost; measure each change. Extraction may still dominate the total expense for a 40–50-page handwritten script.

## Grading-quality acceptance criteria

Use faculty-awarded marks as the reference.

Before running the benchmark, define acceptable thresholds with the domain expert. Do not invent an accuracy threshold after seeing the results.

At minimum, compare:

- Absolute score difference.
- Question-level agreement.
- Partial-credit consistency.
- Correctness of equivalent medical terminology.
- Diagram and label evaluation.
- Handling of missing and unreadable answers.
- Cases where the optimised model awards excessive or insufficient marks.

Investigate material regressions individually. A lower average cost is not an acceptable trade-off if it causes systematic under-grading or over-grading.

Do not deploy an optimised configuration by default until the quality criteria are met.

